%N A396982 Arrange digits in decreasing order in the canonical snowball representation of n.
%C A396982 See A397794 for the definition of the canonical snowball representation of a number.
%C A396982 Unlike similar sequences for fixed bases or factorial base, we don't have a(n) >= n for any n.
%H A396982 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A396982/b396982.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..8192</a>
%H A396982 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A396982/a396982.gp.txt">PARI program</a>
%F A396982 a(a(n)) = a(n).
%e A396982 The first terms are:
%e A396982   n   a(n)  snowball(n)  snowball(a(n))
%e A396982   --  ----  -----------  --------------
%e A396982    0     0          (0)             (0)
%e A396982    1     1            1               1
%e A396982    2     2           10              10
%e A396982    3     3           11              11
%e A396982    4     4          100             100
%e A396982    5     5           21              21
